---
interface Props {
  data: {
    name: string;
    site?: string;
    appStore?: string;
    version: string;
  };
}
const { data } = Astro.props;
const appStore = data.appStore ?? data.site ?? 'https://apps.apple.com/us/app/overhear-meetings/id6781855070?mt=12';
const repo = 'https://github.com/keithah/overhear';
---

<div class="ps-page oh-page">
  <nav class="ps-subnav" aria-label="Overhear sections">
    <a href="#top" class="ps-brand" aria-label="Overhear home">
      <img src="/products/overhear/app-icon.png" alt="" width="26" height="26" />
      <strong>Overhear</strong>
    </a>
    <span class="ps-subnav-links">
      <a href="#features">Features</a>
      <a href="#calendars">Calendars</a>
      <a href="#privacy">Privacy</a>
      <a href="#download">Download</a>
      <a href="#support">Support</a>
    </span>
  </nav>

  <section id="top" class="ps-hero oh-hero" aria-labelledby="overhear-title">
    <div class="ps-hero-copy">
      <p class="ps-eyebrow"><span aria-hidden="true"></span> Free on the Mac App Store</p>
      <h1 id="overhear-title">Your next meeting,<br />one click away.</h1>
      <p class="ps-lede">
        Overhear lives in your menu bar, shows the day's meetings from the calendars already on your
        Mac, and opens the right join link in the right app. No account. No tracking. Nothing leaves
        your machine.
      </p>
      <div class="ps-actions">
        <a class="ps-btn ps-primary" href={appStore} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mac App Store</a>
        <a class="ps-btn ps-ghost" href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ps-release-note">
        macOS 14.0 or later &middot; 2.1 MB &middot; English &middot; rated 4+ &middot; Data Not Collected.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="oh-hero-capture" aria-label="Overhear meeting list and detail panel">
      <img
        class="oh-shot-list"
        src="/products/overhear/meeting-list.png"
        alt="Overhear meeting list with a NOW divider, NEXT badge, platform icons for Zoom, Teams and Meet, and Join buttons."
        width="760"
        height="1040"
        decoding="async"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <img
        class="oh-shot-detail"
        src="/products/overhear/meeting-detail.png"
        alt="Overhear meeting detail panel showing time, organizer, participants and invite notes."
        width="760"
        height="1040"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <div class="oh-menubar" aria-label="Example menu bar item">
        <span>Thu 2:29 PM</span>
        <strong>Design Review</strong>
        <em>in 1m</em>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="features" class="ps-section oh-features" aria-labelledby="oh-features-title">
    <div class="ps-section-head">
      <p class="ps-index">01 / The point</p>
      <div>
        <h2 id="oh-features-title">The gap between meetings is not a place to live.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Finding the link, checking the time, guessing which app it opens in &mdash; Overhear removes all
        of it. The next meeting is in the menu bar, and the click is one click.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="ps-feature-grid oh-feature-grid">
      <article class="ps-card">
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>What's next, at a glance</h3>
        <p>The menu bar carries the next meeting and how long until it starts. Or a generic "Meeting" label when the title shouldn't be on screen.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ps-card">
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>One click, right app</h3>
        <p>Join from the menu bar, the list, a reminder, or a keyboard shortcut. Zoom, Meet, Teams, Webex and dozens more open in their own apps when installed.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ps-card">
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Details before you commit</h3>
        <p>Time, organizer, participants and invite notes, with copy-link, open-in-Calendar, and hide.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ps-card">
        <span>04</span>
        <h3>Reminders that snooze</h3>
        <p>An in-app card or a system notification, cards that fold into a stack you dismiss at once, and optional auto-join when a meeting starts.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figure class="oh-reminder">
      <figcaption>
        <span>Reminders</span>
        <strong>Platform icon, a clear status line, and snooze beside join.</strong>
      </figcaption>
      <img
        src="/products/overhear/reminders.png"
        alt="Overhear reminder cards showing the meeting platform icon, start time, and Join with a Snooze button."
        width="808"
        height="636"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="calendars" class="ps-section oh-calendars" aria-labelledby="oh-calendars-title">
    <div class="ps-section-head">
      <p class="ps-index">02 / Calendars</p>
      <div>
        <h2 id="oh-calendars-title">Your calendars, without giving up an account.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Overhear reads what macOS already syncs. If you'd rather not add an account to Calendar at all,
        add the calendar by its ICS link instead.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="oh-calendar-grid">
      <figure class="oh-calendar-shot">
        <img
          src="/products/overhear/settings-calendars.png"
          alt="Overhear Settings on the Calendars pane, choosing calendars and which events to include."
          width="1520"
          height="1120"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption><span>Settings &middot; Calendars</span> Per-calendar selection, tentative and declined events, all-day events, and regex title filters.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <dl class="oh-calendar-list">
        <div>
          <dt>Calendar links</dt>
          <dd>Add Google, Outlook, Fastmail or iCloud by their ICS link and keep the account out of macOS Calendar. Overhear checks the link and shows how many events are coming up before saving, then refreshes every 15 minutes.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>One meeting, once</dt>
          <dd>When the same meeting arrives from two calendars, it shows once. Turn that off to see every copy.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Colour per calendar</dt>
          <dd>A dot beside each calendar is a colour control &mdash; for linked calendars and macOS ones alike.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Honest about latency</dt>
          <dd>A published Google link can take hours to update. For changes that land in minutes, add the account in System Settings &rarr; Internet Accounts instead.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy" class="ps-section oh-privacy" aria-labelledby="oh-privacy-title">
    <div class="oh-privacy-panel">
      <div>
        <p class="ps-index">03 / Privacy</p>
        <h2 id="oh-privacy-title">Private by design, and specific about it.</h2>
        <p>
          Overhear reads calendars on your Mac and sends nothing anywhere. The App Store privacy label
          says <strong>Data Not Collected</strong>, and meeting titles are kept out of the macOS log.
          Calendar links live in your Keychain &mdash; once added, Overhear shows only the address,
          never the full link.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="oh-facts">
        <div><dt>Account</dt><dd>None</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Tracking</dt><dd>None</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Price</dt><dd>Free</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Size</dt><dd>2.1 MB</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="download" class="ps-section oh-download" aria-labelledby="oh-download-title">
    <div class="ps-download-panel">
      <div>
        <p class="ps-index">04 / Download</p>
        <h2 id="oh-download-title">Install it and forget it's there.</h2>
        <p>
          Free on the Mac App Store for macOS 14.0 or later. A separate direct-download build adds
          Sparkle updates for people who'd rather not use the store.
        </p>
        <div class="ps-actions">
          <a class="ps-btn ps-primary" href={appStore} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mac App Store</a>
          <a class="ps-btn" href={`${repo}/releases`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Direct download</a>
          <a class="ps-btn ps-ghost" href={`${repo}/blob/main/README.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">README</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="ps-release-box">
        <dt>Requires</dt>
        <dd>macOS 14.0+</dd>
        <dt>Category</dt>
        <dd>Productivity</dd>
        <dt>Languages</dt>
        <dd>English</dd>
        <dt>Age rating</dt>
        <dd>4+</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="support" class="ps-section oh-support" aria-labelledby="oh-support-title">
    <div class="ps-support-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 id="oh-support-title">Something not joining?</h2>
        <p>
          Open an issue on GitHub with your macOS version, the service, and what you expected to
          happen. Overhear is a one-person project, so a reproducible report is the fastest path.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="ps-actions">
        <a class="ps-btn ps-primary" href={`${repo}/issues/new`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open an issue</a>
        <a class="ps-btn" href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Browse the code</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
